PROJECT CONTEXT — mock3

Two sibling TypeScript codelines, each self-contained, ESM (`"type": "module"`), no shared package and no cross-imports:

- mocka — /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a — `src/fares.ts`, `test/fares.test.ts`. Owns MOCK3-1 (riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare). Exports `Rider`, `BASE_FARE_CENTS`, `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS`, `fareFor`, `totalFor`.
- mockb — /home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b — `src/schedule.ts`, `test/schedule.test.ts`. Owns MOCK3-2 (the departure board omits the final stop). Exports `Stop`, `formatStops`, `totalMinutes`.

Each codeline declares exactly two devDependencies: `vitest` and `typescript`. Nothing else. Scripts in both `package.json` files: `npm test` → `vitest run`, `npm run build` → `tsc --noEmit`, `npm run lint` → a stub that echoes `no-lint-configured`. Treat any acceptance criterion that assumes a linter, a runtime dependency, a build artefact, a database, a server, an HTTP layer, a fixture format, a snapshot tool or a second test framework as introducing a technology this project does not already use — one `cat package.json` in the owning codeline settles it.

WHERE TO VERIFY A CLAIM

A fare claim (age thresholds, pass handling, cents amounts, totals) resolves against `mock-a/src/fares.ts`; its current coverage against `mock-a/test/fares.test.ts`. A departure-board claim (which stops are rendered, separator, minute formatting, trip length) resolves against `mock-b/src/schedule.ts`; its current coverage against `mock-b/test/schedule.test.ts`. Both source files are short enough to read whole — one `cat` is the whole verification, so a "cannot be verified by reading source code" rejection on a fare or schedule fact is almost always wrong. Do not go looking in the other codeline: mocka has no schedule code and mockb has no fare code.

TRAPS IN THIS STACK

Both tickets are single-line boundary bugs in pure functions — a comparison operator in `fareFor` and a loop bound in `formatStops`. That makes off-by-one wording the thing to read most carefully:

- "65 and over" vs "over 65", and `>=` vs `>`, are different rules. A criterion is only unverifiable if the source genuinely does not encode the boundary; if the operator is right there in `fareFor`, it is verifiable. Note that `src/fares.ts` also carries a doc comment stating the intended rule, which may or may not agree with the code — the code is the fact, the comment is a claim.
- `formatStops` iterates with an explicit bound; whether the last element is included is readable from that bound alone. `totalMinutes` reads the last stop and is a separate function — a criterion about trip duration is not a criterion about rendering.
- Fares are integer cents constants, not currency strings or floats. An AFTER that introduces formatted money, rounding or a locale is adding behaviour the codeline does not have.
- These are pure functions with no I/O, no clock and no config. An AFTER that makes a criterion depend on environment, ordering across runs or test-runner internals is untestable here, not just awkward.

